<%# NOT SHIPPED YET, and this page says so before it says anything else.
    The feature is designed and largely built; what it is not is available, and a
    marketing page that reads as though it were would be the one claim on this
    site that a reader could disprove by signing up. The waitlist is what
    replaces the "Start measuring" call to action until it ships. -%>
<% seo title: "Revenue attribution (coming soon) · Tastatur",
       description: "Coming soon: connect Stripe read-only and see which acquisition channel produced " \
                    "paying customers — first-touch, write-once, with no cookies and no join to any " \
                    "visitor identifier. Join the list to hear when it ships.",
       og_type: "article",
       markdown: revenue_path(format: :md) %>

<div class="shell">
  <%# --- Hero -%>
  <section class="rule-heavy pt-8 pb-14">
    <p class="label mb-5">Coming soon · first-touch · Stripe, read-only</p>
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3.25rem)] font-semibold leading-[1.06] tracking-[-0.035em] max-w-3xl">
      Know which channel produces customers.<br>
      <span class="text-signal">Not just visitors.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-ink-2 mt-6 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
      Every analytics tool can rank where your traffic comes from. The question a business
      actually needs answered is which of those channels produced the people who pay — and
      the channel sending the most visitors is very often not the one sending the most
      customers.
    </p>

    <%# Said plainly and high up rather than in a footnote. Everything below this
        line describes a design, in the present tense because that is how a design
        reads — so the tense has to be set here, once, unambiguously. -%>
    <div class="notice mt-8 max-w-2xl">
      <strong>This is not available yet.</strong> Everything below describes what it will do
      and the decisions already made about it. Nothing on this page is measuring anything
      today. Join the list and we will email you once, when it ships.
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-xl">
      <%= render "waitlist_signups/form", source: "revenue_page" %>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 mt-8">
      <%= link_to "Read the integration docs", docs_path(anchor: "attribution"), class: "btn",
                  data: { analytics_event: "CTA Clicked",
                          analytics_props: { cta: "attribution-docs", placement: "revenue-hero" } } %>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# --- The example report. Static illustration, labelled as such: the point
      it makes — most visitors ≠ most revenue — is the whole feature. -%>
  <section class="py-14 border-t border-rule">
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 mb-6 max-w-3xl">
      <p class="label">What the attribution report answers</p>
      <p class="label">Example figures</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card max-w-3xl">
      <div class="card-head">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold">Channels</h2>
        <span class="label">Sorted by MRR — deliberately</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <%# Short headers below sm, same convention as the goals tables: the
            full words at the label's tracking are wider than any column a
            phone can afford. %>
        <div class="bar-row label border-b border-rule">
          <span>Channel</span>
          <span class="flex gap-2 sm:gap-8">
            <span class="w-16 sm:w-20 text-right"><span class="sm:hidden">Visit.</span><span class="hidden sm:inline">Visitors</span></span>
            <span class="w-16 sm:w-20 text-right"><span class="sm:hidden">Sign.</span><span class="hidden sm:inline">Signups</span></span>
            <span class="w-16 sm:w-20 text-right">MRR</span>
          </span>
        </div>
        <% [
             ["Reddit",  "412",   "38", "$1,240", 100],
             ["Google",  "2,180", "21", "$340",   27],
             ["Direct",  "960",   "12", "$180",   15],
             ["X (Twitter)", "1,470", "9", "$90",  7]
           ].each do |channel, visitors, signups, mrr, width| %>
          <div class="bar-row">
            <span class="bar-fill" style="width: <%= width %>%"></span>
            <span class="bar-label font-medium"><%= channel %></span>
            <span class="flex gap-2 sm:gap-8 num">
              <span class="w-16 sm:w-20 text-right"><%= visitors %></span>
              <span class="w-16 sm:w-20 text-right"><%= signups %></span>
              <span class="w-16 sm:w-20 text-right font-semibold"><%= mrr %></span>
            </span>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-[13px] text-muted mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
      Sorted by money rather than by visitors, because sorting by visitors reliably puts the
      channel that sends crowds at the top — here, the search traffic — while the channel
      that sends customers sits three rows down looking small.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- The design: two ledgers that never meet -%>
  <section class="py-14 border-t border-rule">
    <p class="label mb-8">Two ledgers, joined only on a channel name</p>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-px bg-rule max-w-4xl">
      <div class="bg-surface p-7">
        <h2 class="font-semibold">The traffic ledger stays anonymous</h2>
        <p class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          Visits are counted the way Tastatur always counts them: no cookies, nothing stored
          on the device, a visitor identifier that stops working after 24 hours. The referrer
          is kept as a hostname only and classified into a channel the moment it is written.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-surface p-7">
        <h2 class="font-semibold">The revenue ledger is your own customer data</h2>
        <p class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          Customers, subscriptions and payments are data your application already holds.
          Your server sends them to Tastatur directly — authenticated, server-to-server,
          under your own relationship with your customers. Email addresses are hashed on
          receipt and never stored in the clear.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 mt-6 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
      <strong class="text-ink">There is no column joining the two.</strong> Nothing links a
      customer to a visitor identifier — not at collection, not later. The two ledgers meet
      only in the report, on channel names: all the visitors on one side, all the money on
      the other. That is a design constraint, not a limitation we apologise for, and because
      the source is AGPL-3.0 you can verify it rather than take our word.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- The three integration points, with the same code the docs show -%>
  <section class="py-14 border-t border-rule">
    <p class="label mb-3">The integration, in three steps</p>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-ink-2 mb-8 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
      The browser knows where somebody came from; the payment happens somewhere else,
      possibly days later, possibly on another device. With nothing stored on the visitor's
      device there is no cookie to join the two — so the attribution travels <em>with</em>
      the signup and the payment instead.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-10 max-w-3xl">
      <div>
        <p class="text-[14px] leading-relaxed mb-3">
          <strong>1. Capture the first touch on the landing page.</strong> Reads the campaign
          tags and referrer of the current visit. Store it in your own form state or user
          record — calling it at the end of your funnel would read your own checkout as the
          traffic source.
        </p>
        <%= render "docs/snippet", language: "JavaScript",
                   code: %(const attribution = tastatur.attribution()\n// { source, medium, campaign, landing_path, referrer_host, first_seen_at }) %>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="text-[14px] leading-relaxed mb-3">
          <strong>2. Send it when they sign up,</strong> from your server, with an API key
          from your site's settings. The email is hashed on receipt.
        </p>
        <%= render "docs/snippet", language: "HTTP",
                   code: %(POST #{Tastatur.base_url}/api/v1/identify\nAuthorization: Bearer tk_...\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  "external_id": "user_9182",\n  "email": "person@example.com",\n  "attribution": { "source": "reddit", "medium": "social", "landing_path": "/pricing" }\n}) %>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="text-[14px] leading-relaxed mb-3">
          <strong>3. Attach it to Stripe Checkout.</strong> This is what makes attribution
          survive a closed tab, or a payment finished three days later on a phone: the
          first touch rides inside the checkout session's metadata and comes back to us on
          the payment webhook.
        </p>
        <%= render "docs/snippet", language: "JavaScript",
                   code: %(stripe.checkout.sessions.create({\n  mode: 'subscription',\n  line_items: [...],\n  client_reference_id: user.id,\n  metadata: tastatur.checkoutMetadata(attribution)\n})) %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 mt-8 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
      <strong class="text-ink">First touch wins, and is never overwritten.</strong> Your app
      calls identify on every sign-in, so a last-write-wins design would re-attribute
      January's Reddit customer to whatever they searched in March — and every paid channel
      would decay towards "Direct" on its own. Attribution is written once; only an earlier
      first touch can amend it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# --- Stripe, read-only -%>
  <section class="py-14 border-t border-rule">
    <p class="label mb-3">Connect Stripe, read-only</p>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-ink-2 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
      Connecting Stripe imports your history and keeps revenue flowing in as it happens —
      subscriptions, invoices, refunds, disputes. The connection is a Stripe App whose
      permissions are all read: Stripe shows you each one, item by item, before you install,
      and its review process pins the list. There is no code path here that writes to a
      connected Stripe account, and no access token is stored on our side — you can
      disconnect from either end, and revenue already recorded stays yours to keep or erase.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-px bg-rule max-w-3xl mt-7">
      <% [
           ["read", "the only kind of permission the app asks for"],
           ["0", "access tokens stored on our side"],
           ["2 yrs", "of history imported on connect"],
           ["1 click", "to disconnect, from either end"]
         ].each do |value, note| %>
        <div class="bg-surface px-5 py-5">
          <p class="figure text-[26px]"><%= value %></p>
          <p class="text-[12px] text-ink-2 mt-2 leading-snug"><%= note %></p>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# --- The honesty block. This feature is the identifiable part of the
      product, and saying so plainly is the trust argument. -%>
  <section class="py-14 border-t border-rule">
    <p class="label mb-8">Said plainly</p>
    <div class="space-y-5 max-w-3xl text-[14px] leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        <strong>This part of Tastatur is not anonymous, and does not pretend to be.</strong>
        A customer record carries the identifier your app assigned, a hashed email, and a
        first-touch channel. That is data your application already holds about somebody who
        signed up for it, confined to five tables, deletable when you say so.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Your visitors are not in it.</strong> Someone who browses and never signs up
        exists only in the anonymous ledger, under an identifier that stopped working the
        day they visited.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Revenue never appears on a public shared dashboard.</strong> A shared link
        shows traffic, never money. Publishing MRR to an unguessable-but-public URL is not
        something anybody should be one checkbox away from.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>It works self-hosted.</strong> Revenue attribution is deliberately not tied
        to our billing: it is about whether you can connect your processor, not whether we
        can charge you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# --- CTA. The second copy of the form, for a reader who arrived at the top
      unconvinced and got to the bottom convinced — which is the whole reason a
      long page repeats its call to action. Same partial, different `source`, so
      the two placements can be told apart without the copy drifting. -%>
  <section class="py-14 border-t border-rule">
    <p class="label mb-5">Hear about it when it ships</p>
    <div class="max-w-xl">
      <%= render "waitlist_signups/form", source: "revenue_page_footer" %>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 mt-8">
      <%= link_to "Integration docs", docs_path(anchor: "attribution"), class: "btn" %>
      <%= link_to "What gets collected", privacy_path, class: "btn btn-quiet" %>
      <% if Tastatur.allow_signup? %>
        <%# Signing up is still the right action for the product that DOES exist,
            demoted to a quiet link so it cannot read as "start measuring
            revenue". -%>
        <%= link_to "Start measuring traffic", new_user_registration_path, class: "btn btn-quiet",
                    data: { analytics_event: "CTA Clicked",
                            analytics_props: { cta: "start-measuring", placement: "revenue-footer" } } %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
